From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ep9
Subject: [PATCH] Take only ASCII hex digits in a \u escape

A RegExpUnicodeEscapeSequence has only HexDigits (ECMA-262 22.2.1,
12.9.3). The parser read the digits of \u{...}, of \uXXXX and of the
trail surrogate escape with from_str_radix, which also takes a leading
+ sign. So /\u+0ff/ matched U+00FF, /\u{+1F600}/u was U+1F600 and
/(?<a\u+041>x)/ was the group name aA. In node, /\u+0ff/ is the
identity escape u, then +, then 0ff (Annex B), /[\u+fff-z]/ is a valid
class, and /\u{+1F600}/u and /(?<a\u+041>x)/ are SyntaxErrors.

The parser now checks that the digits are ASCII hex digits before each
from_str_radix call. The group-name paths use the same function.

In Unicode mode, u or v, such a \u is a SyntaxError. The flag string "v"
sets only unicode_sets, not unicode, so the identity escape check also
reads unicode_sets: with "v" and the hex check alone, /[\u+fff-z]/ was the
class u, +, f-z (node: a SyntaxError).

Also: when a lead surrogate escape is not followed by a trail surrogate
escape, the parser restored the input to after the second \u, so the
next escape lost its \u: /\uD83D\u0041/ matched U+D83D then "0041"
(node: U+D83D then A), and /\uD83D\uzzzz/u was valid (node: a
SyntaxError). The input now goes back to the second \u.

Test: tests/unicode_escape_hex.rs.
